LIMITS AND TRADE-OFFS I MUST BE ABLE TO EXPLAIN

- No cross-table ACID transaction; retries rely on safe per-table behavior and reconciliation.
- Main sample tables still contain tiny files because 1000 records are spread through a year. Daily pruning has a metadata/file-count trade-off.
- Compaction results are for an isolated table, not proof main-table file layout was fixed.
- One immutable ride record per ID. Multiple event versions or corrections need a different design.
- The current fare type is double with tolerance checks, not a production fixed-decimal accounting design.
- No driver/rider master FK enforcement, arbitrary retroactive city correction, full concurrent-writer coordination, cloud deployment or streaming watermark engine.
- Spark SQL scan metrics vary; files emitting rows and their filesystem lengths are not physical I/O measurements.
- Current orchestration reads stored tables and is not optimized to process only changed records throughout every stage.
- Synthetic labels describe injected conditions and are separate from runtime decisions.
- SHA-256 entity/batch identifiers are different from the non-cryptographic replay-table fingerprints.

